%\VignetteIndexEntry{Permutation of constraints and unequal whole plots}
\documentclass[a4paper,10pt]{article}
\usepackage{vegan}
\title{Permutation of constraints and unequal whole plots}
\author{vegan development version}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\SweaveOpts{strip.white=true}
<<setup,echo=FALSE>>=
library("vegan")
options(width=70)
set.seed(42)
@
\section{Stored constraint matrices}
The option \verb|model = "constraints"| jointly permutes rows of the fitted
constraint matrix. It works with \verb|anova()| for RDA, CCA, dbRDA and
\verb|capscale()|, and directly with \verb|adonis2()|. Factor coding,
interactions and fitted transformation columns move together. The response
working matrix is fixed. Each draw recomputes centering, CCA row weights,
the constraint QR decomposition and the pseudo-$F$ statistic. The original
formula data are unnecessary for this calculation.

\section{Unequal whole plots}
The accompanying permute design supplies plot identifiers and one or more
plot-level variables at the original sample-row level. All selected variables
must be constant within plots. Complete variable rows define the distinct
arrangements through ordered set partitions, preserving treatment-combination
counts in plots while allowing sample counts to change.
<<unequal>>=
sizes <- c(2, 5, 3, 4)
dat <- data.frame(
    plot = rep(LETTERS[1:4], sizes),
    treatment = factor(rep(c("T", "C", "T", "C"), sizes))
)
Y <- matrix(rexp(nrow(dat) * 5), nrow(dat), 5)
h <- how(
    plots = Plots(~ plot, type = "free", variables = ~ treatment),
    data = dat
)
numPerms(nrow(dat), h)
draws <- allPerms(nrow(dat), update(h, observed = TRUE))
sort(rowSums(permutedVariables(draws) == "T"))
fit <- rda(Y ~ treatment, data = dat)
anova(fit, model = "constraints", permutations = h)
D <- vegdist(Y, method = "bray")
adonis2(D ~ treatment, data = dat,
        model = "constraints", permutations = h)
@
The same call to \verb|anova()| works with models fitted by \verb|cca()|,
\verb|dbrda()| or \verb|capscale()|. Ordinary observation-index matrices and
existing \verb|how()| designs also work with this model option.

\section{Joint factor combinations}
For a factorial whole-plot design, list all variables needed to distinguish
the constraint rows. For example, use \verb|variables = ~ A + B| with a fitted
\verb|Y ~ A * B| model. Two plots per combination in a two-by-two design give
$8!/(2!)^4=2520$ distinct arrangements, including the observed one. Balanced
and unbalanced sample replication use the same design.

These permutations give an overall test. Separate main-effect tests,
interaction tests, contrast tests and nested-model comparisons need
appropriate null-specific procedures. The current implementation supports
\verb|by = NULL| and the ordinary pseudo-$F$ statistic. A variable-based
whole-plot design requires \verb|Within(type = "none")|. Ordinary row designs
can use within-plot shuffling where their exchangeability assumptions hold.

\section{Conditioning variables and interpretation}
\verb|Condition(Z)| leaves the conditioning matrix fixed. The implementation
reports an informational message that nuisance adjustment alone does not justify exchangeability of the
constraint rows. A randomized experiment can use fixed baseline covariates
when the permutations reproduce its actual randomization, including
blocking. Free constraint shuffling generally fails for observational
predictors associated with the conditioning variables. The exact
randomization argument concerns a sharp null of no treatment effect.

\section{Enumeration and retained observations}
Designs supplied through \verb|how()| are aligned with the observations retained
by a fitted model before permutations are generated. Precomputed permutations
must already match that layout. Set partitions omit rearrangements of
identical complete variable rows. The selected variables must distinguish
all distinct constraint rows in the fit.

Enumeration excludes the observed arrangement by default, giving the usual
plus-one p-value. When the supplied complete enumeration explicitly includes
the observed arrangement, it is counted directly. Tied statistics are included
in both cases.
\end{document}
